==English==

===Etymology===
A reverse spelling of {{m|en|palindromes}}. "Semordnilap", according to author O. V.<!-- Full name unknown, puzzle columnist for the Mensa International Journal --> Michaelsen in his 1997 book ''Words at Play'', was probably first used by recreational linguist [[w:Dmitri Borgmann|Dmitri Borgmann]], cited by [[w:Martin Gardner|Martin Gardner]] in the revised edition of Charles Carroll Bombaugh's ''Oddities and Curiosities of Words and Literature'' (1961) [http://books.google.com/books?id=f4kNAQAAIAAJ&q=borgmann]. The underlying concept (but not the term) is found at least as far back as [[w:Lewis Carroll|Lewis Carroll]]'s ''Sylvie and Bruno'' (1889).

===Noun===
{{examples|examples=
* [[desserts]] → [[stressed]]
* [[diaper]] → [[repaid]]
* [[live]] → [[evil]]}}
{{en-noun}}

# A word, phrase, or sentence that has the property of forming another word, phrase, or sentence when its letters are reversed. A semordnilap differs from a [[palindrome]] in that the word or phrase resulting from the reversal is different from the original word or phrase.
#* {{quote-book|en|1965|[[w:Dmitri Borgmann|Dmitri Borgmann]]|Language on Vacation: An Olio of Orthological Oddities|page=42|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=OMpAAAAAIAAJ
|passage=[he] then goes on to credit us with giving him some of the best examples of '''semordnilaps'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|2000|Gail Smith|Celebrate the Piano|page=18|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=3gmlW_hW4JMC&pg=PA18
|passage=This song is a '''semordnilap'''. It can be played forward and then sounds different when it is played backwards.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|2008|Alex Pogel & David Ozonoff|chapter=Contingency Structures and Concept Analysis|title=Formal Concept Analysis|editors=Medina & Obietkov|page=318|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=J3mTaeAzEAAC&pg=PA318
|passage=ACE is an acronym for Analysis of Concepts for Epidemiologists, and a '''semordnilap''' for ECA, Epidemiological Concept Analysis.}}

